    "innovation": "Using a 17-model ensemble, this paper quantifies the Time of Emergence for ocean biogeochemical trends, finding that signals like pCO2 and pH emerge decades faster than sea surface temperature and that natural variability is more important than trend strength in controlling their detectability.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Equatorial Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "To evaluate N, we can verify the presence of main characteristics of natural variability. One prominent feature is El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (ENSO; Fiedler, 2002), located in the equatorial Pacific. This climate mode, the most important factor concerning natural variability in the climate system on global scales, is known to have substantial impact on the ocean carbon cycle in the affected area (e.g., Le Qu\u00e9r\u00e9 et al., 2010; Wanninkhof et al., 2013) and the global air-sea CO2 flux in general (Siegenthaler, 1990; McKinley et al., 2004). We find clear indications of ENSO in the SD patterns of SST, DIC and pCO2, and a weak signal for pH.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Another area of high natural variability is the North Atlantic, which is influenced by modes like the North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO; Hurrell and Deser, 2009) or changes in the Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation (AMOC; Carton and H\u00e4kkinen, 2011), both known for affecting the ocean carbon cycle (e.g., Keller et al., 2012; Perez et al., 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Meridional Overturning Circulation",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "An illustrative example is the slowdown of the AMOC, which was suggested by Bryden et al. (2005) based on five cross sections in the North Atlantic. These snap-shot measurements were distributed over the seasons in such a way that they embraced the full range of the seasonal cycle, and the observed slow-down was later attributed mainly to \"aliasing due to seasonal anomalies\u201d (Kanzow et al., 2010).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Equatorial Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "An exception is the eastern equatorial Pacific, which is strongly influenced by high ENSO-related natural variability.",
